e where we'll just  walk away and somehow our consciences  are clear  uh quite the opposite this is a much  deeper commitment to walking  along the path of ensuring that  communities have  safe access to clean and reliable  drinking water  for the long term  97 first nations water advisories have  been lifted since 2015  59 are still in effect and it's expected  at least 22 will remain past  march of 2021. one of the world's most  lucrative fisheries opens this week in  southwest nova scotia  lobster fishers land a huge catch for  export worth  almost three billion dollars a year it's  a reminder how precious  and how resource rich the world's oceans  are  and as ross lord reports canada has now  teamed up with other nations  to protect them the health of oceans has  been declining for decades  from activities like overfishing and  pollution taking what we want  and putting back in what we don't want  in canada research shows only a quarter  of fish stocks is reliably healthy  a new international effort promises to  elevate a healthy ocean from the bottom  of the global policy agenda  to the top through this commitment each  country  will have a sustainable ocean plan  in place by 2025.  the country's promised to improve nearly  30 million square kilometers  of national waters an area the size of  africa  if it happens they say oceans will  produce more food more renewable energy  and ease global warming canada and 13  partner nations have signed on among  those who haven't  is the united states their leader at the  time which was donald trump didn't have  much of an interest in joining this  agenda  maybe under the new administration we do  see a shift in thinking there  the leaders say they're not trying to  divert attention away from urgent  priorities like stopping covet 19  they insist the ocean can bring economic  relief as the world looks to recover  from the pandemic  a statement from prime minister trudeau  reinforces canada's support  but some of the panel